|> Chapter
    title=Bibliographic notes

    Here are the main sources I used during the preparation of these lecture notes. I had a lot of fun consulting these books, but had to leave out many beautiful topics. The interested reader is encouraged to continue his or her journey into the world of ODEs by looking into these more detailed sources. I especially recommend the books by Arnold, Banks and anything written by Richard Feynman (including his hilarious memoir _Surely You're Joking, Mr. Feynman_).

    *Sources for Part 1*

    |> ol
        class=list
        data-list-style=decimal

        |> li
            V. I. Arnold, Mathematical Methods of Classical Mechanics, 2nd Ed. Springer, 1988.

        |> li
            R. B. Banks, Towing Icebergs, Falling Dominoes, and Other Adventures in Applied Mathematics. Princeton University Press, 1998.

        |> li
            R. Grimshaw, Nonlinear Ordinary Differential Equations. Blackwell Scientific Publications, 1990.

        |> li
            J. K. Hunter, [Lecture Notes on Applied Mathematics](http://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/m280_09/applied_math.html), available online.

        |> li
            L. A. Pars, An Introduction to the Calculus of Variations. Heinemann Educational Books, 1962 (reprinted by Dover Publications, 2010).

    *Sources for Part 2*

    |> ol
        class=list
        data-list-style=decimal
        start=6
        style=--list-start: 5

        |> li
            D. Romik, Lecture Notes for Math 235B: Probability Theory, available online at [http://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~romik/teaching/mat235-2011-2012/#235b](http://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~romik/teaching/mat235-2011-2012/#235b)

        |> li
            S. H. Strogatz, Nonlinear Dynamics and Chaos. Westview Press, 1994.

    *Sources for Part 3*

    |> ol
        class=list
        data-list-style=decimal
        start=8
        style=--list-start: 7

        |> li
            Chapter 5 of Arnold's book listed above.

        |> li
            R. P. Feynman, R. B. Leighton, M. Sands, The Feynman Lectures on Physics, Vol. II. Basic Books, 1964. (Chapter 29 has a discussion of alternating-gradient focusing and the inverted pendulum with an oscillating base.)

        |> li
            O. L. R. Jacobs, Introduction to Control Theory. Oxford Science Publications, 1993.
